data: {"type":"token","content":"# PLTR vs. LMT, Last 3 Years at a Glance\n\n**Bottom line:** Over the past three fiscal years (FY2023, FY2025), Palantir has been the growth story and Lockheed Martin has been the scale/cash-return story. PLTR roughly doubled revenue and swung from thin profits to ~32% operating margins, while LMT posted low-single-digit revenue growth and slightly compressing margins, but generated many multiples of PLTR's dollar profits and cash returned to shareholders. As of the Sept. 2, 2026 close, PLTR trades at a market cap of ~$389B on a P/E of ~144, versus LMT at ~$123B on a P/E of ~20.\n\n## What each company is\n- **PLTR (Palantir):** Software / data analytics platforms (Gotham, Foundry, AIP) for government and enterprise. ~4,400 employees.\n- **LMT (Lockheed Martin):** Aerospace & defense prime contractor (F-35, missiles, rotorcraft, space). ~123,000 employees.\n\n## Market snapshot (as of Sept. 2, 2026 close)\n\n| Metric | PLTR | LMT |\n|---|---|---|\n| Price | $169.46 | $531.60 |\n| Market cap | ~$389.1B | ~$122.7B |\n| P/E (TTM) | 143.6 | 19.6 |\n| EPS (TTM) | $1.20 | $26.48 |\n| 52-week range | $106.37, $207.52 | $437.25, $692.00 |\n| Dividend yield | 0% | ~3% |\n\n## Revenue and profitability (fiscal year, ended Dec. 31)\n\n| $ millions | PLTR 2023 | PLTR 2024 | PLTR 2025 | LMT 2023 | LMT 2024 | LMT 2025 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | 2,225 | 2,866 | 4,475 | 67,571 | 71,043 | 75,048 |\n| Gross profit | 1,794 | 2,300 | 3,686 | 8,479 | 6,930 | 7,619 |\n| Operating income | 120 | 310 | 1,414 | 8,507 | 7,013 | 7,731 |\n| Net income | 217 | 468 | 1,635 | 6,920 | 5,336 | 5,017 |\n| Diluted EPS | $0.09 | $0.19 | $0.63 | $27.55 | $22.31 | $21.49 |\n| Operating margin | 5% | 11% | 32% | 13% | 10% | 10% |\n| Gross margin | 81% | 80% | 82% | 13% | 10% | 10% |\n\nKey takeaways:\n- **Growth:** PLTR revenue grew from $2.2B \u2192 $4.5B (roughly 2x in three years). LMT went $67.6B \u2192 $75.0B (~11% cumulative).\n- **Margin trajectory:** PLTR's operating margin expanded from ~5% to ~32%. LMT's edged down from ~13% to ~10%, partly reflecting charges in its Aeronautics and classified programs in recent years.\n- **Absolute dollars:** LMT still earns ~3x PLTR's net income in dollars despite far lower growth.\n\n## Cash flow and shareholder returns ($ millions)\n\n| Item | PLTR 2023 | PLTR 2024 | PLTR 2025 | LMT 2023 | LMT 2024 | LMT 2025 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cash from operations | 712 | 1,154 | 2,134 | 7,920 | 6,972 | 8,557 |\n| Capex | -15 | -13 | -34 | -1,691 | -1,685 | -1,649 |\n| Free cash flow (approx.) | 697 | 1,141 | 2,100 | 6,229 | 5,287 | 6,908 |\n| Dividends paid | 0 | 0 | 0 | -3,056 | -3,059 | -3,131 |\n| Share buybacks | 0 | -64 | -75 | -6,000 | -3,700 | -3,000 |\n\n- LMT returned roughly **$9B+ per year** to shareholders via dividends and buybacks; PLTR pays no dividend and has done only small buybacks (mostly to offset dilution).\n- PLTR is nearly capex-free (software), which is why FCF conversion is very high; LMT reinvests ~$1.6-1.7B/year in property/plant.\n\n## Balance sheet ($ millions, end of FY)\n\n| Item | PLTR 2023 | PLTR 2025 | LMT 2023 | LMT 2025 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Total assets | 4,522 | 8,900 | 52,456 | 59,840 |\n| Cash & equivalents | 831 | 1,424 | 1,442 | 4,121 |\n| Shareholders' equity | 3,561 | 7,488 | 6,835 | 6,721 |\n\nPLTR has essentially no debt and a growing cash pile. LMT carries meaningful long-term debt (not shown above, data unavailable in the standardized total-debt field for these periods) and a relatively thin equity base because of its heavy buyback history.\n\n## How to think about them side by side\n- **PLTR** = high-growth software with expanding margins, no dividend, and a valuation (P/E ~144) that already prices in continued rapid growth.\n- **LMT** = mature defense prime with modest top-line growth, stable ~10% margins, a ~3% dividend, and heavy buybacks, valued like a slow-growth industrial (P/E ~20).\n\nThey compete for the same government dollars in some pockets (software, AI, C2/ISR), but they are fundamentally different businesses, one is a software platform, the other a hardware-heavy systems integrator. This is not investment advice."}

