hree years). LMT went $67.6B \u2192 $75.0B (~11% cumulative).\n- **Margin trajectory:** PLTR's operating margin expanded from ~5% to ~32%. LMT's edged down from ~13% to ~10%, partly reflecting charges in its Aeronautics and classified programs in recent years.\n- **Absolute dollars:** LMT still earns ~3x PLTR's net income in dollars despite far lower growth.\n\n## Cash flow and shareholder returns ($ millions)\n\n| Item | PLTR 2023 | PLTR 2024 | PLTR 2025 | LMT 2023 | LMT 2024 | LMT 2025 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cash from operations | 712 | 1,154 | 2,134 | 7,920 | 6,972 | 8,557 |\n| Capex | -15 | -13 | -34 | -1,691 | -1,685 | -1,649 |\n| Free cash flow (approx.) | 697 | 1,141 | 2,100 | 6,229 | 5,287 | 6,908 |\n| Dividends paid | 0 | 0 | 0 | -3,056 | -3,059 | -3,131 |\n| Share buybacks | 0 | -64 | -75 | -6,000 | -3,700 | -3,000 |\n\n- LMT returned roughly **$9B+ per year** to shareholders via dividends and buybacks; PLTR pays no dividend and has done only small buybacks (mostly to offset dilution).\n- PLTR is nearly capex-free (software), which is why FCF conversion is very high; LMT reinvests ~$1.6-1.7B/year in property/plant.\n\n## Balance sheet ($ millions, end of FY)\n\n| Item | PLTR 2023 | PLTR 2025 | LMT 2023 | LMT 2025 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Total assets | 4,522 | 8,900 | 52,456 | 59,840 |\n| Cash & equivalents | 831 | 1,424 | 1,442 | 4,121 |\n| Shareholders' equity | 3,561 | 7,488 | 6,835 | 6,721 |\n\nPLTR has essentially no debt and a growing cash pile. LMT carries meaningful long-term debt (not shown above, data unavailable in the standardized total-debt field for these periods) and a relatively thin equity base because of its heavy buyback history.\n\n## How to think about them side by side\n- **PLTR** = high-growth software with expanding margins, no dividend, and a valuation (P/E ~144) that already prices in continued rapid growth.\n- **LMT** = mature defense prime with modest top-line growth, stable ~10% margins, a ~3% dividend, and heavy buybacks, valued like a slow-growth industrial (P/E ~20).\n\nThey compete for the same government dollars in some pockets (software, AI, C2/ISR), but they are fundamentally different businesses, one is a software platform, the other a hardware-heavy systems integrator. This is not investment advice.","research":{"schema_version":"argus_research/v1","capability_class":"thorough_research","shape":"thorough","sources":[],"retrieved_at":"2026-09-03T05:35:12.463851+00:00","anchor_symbols":["PLTR","LMT"],"peers":[],"usage":{"invocations":3,"latency_ms":37139,"cost_usd":0.19487,"cache_status":"miss"},"follow_up":{"schema_version":"argus_research_follow_up/v1","subjects":[{"symbol":"PLTR","name":"Palantir Technologies Inc. Class A Common Stock","asset_class":"equity"},{"symbol":"LMT","name":"Lockheed Martin Corp.","asset_class":"equity"}],"comparison_set":["PLTR","LMT"],"peer_suggestions":[],"open_thread":{"shape":"thorough","period_of_interest":"last 3 years"}}},"next_experiments":{"version":"argus_next_experiments/v1","rows":[{"kind":"research_test_versus","label":"Test Palantir Technologies Inc. Class A (PLTR) vs Lockheed Martin (LMT) over the last 3 years","label_parts":[{"type":"text","value":"Test "},{"type":"text","value":"Palantir Technologies Inc. Class A"},{"type":"ticker","value":"PLTR"},{"type":"text","value":" vs "},{"type":"text","value":"Lockheed Martin"},{"type":"ticker","value":"LMT"},{"type":"text","value":" over the last 3 years"}],"label_key":"chat.next_experiments.labels.research_dynamic","send_text":"Test buying and holding PLTR, LMT over the last three years"}]},"message_id":"c31be3b8-76e1-4b31-9bb6-b00ab9920212"}}

